### Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint

A producer that hands off inside a managed workspace publishes a **Change
Request**: a revisioned record attached to the existing Task/Bug/Story, not a new
board element. It carries the branch, the base and candidate tree OIDs, a binary
patch resource with its SHA-256, the changed-path list, and an explicit
`repository_delta` of `present` or `empty`.

The candidate is snapshotted through a temporary alternate Git index, so tracked
changes, deletions, and untracked non-ignored files are all captured **without
touching the managed worktree's own index**. Every `.task-board/**` entry is then
restored from the base tree, which is why a `changed_paths` list can never
contain a board path — the authoritative board is the control root, and a
worktree's `.task-board` copy is a checkout artifact. Paths dropped by that rule
are recorded as `dropped_board_paths` and shown by `worktree status` and in the
reviewer's prompt. A non-empty list is a diagnostic, usually a `git add -A` in
the child, not a failure.

Construction runs for **every** producer completion in a managed workspace,
whether or not the run carries a delivery goal. Managed workspace activation
turns on role, task class, containing Story and local board alone, so an ordinary
`task-board spawn TASK-... --role developer --background` gets an isolated
worktree — and the half of the feature that makes that worktree reviewable
activates on exactly the same conditions.

It runs **after** the spawn completion artifact guard, and on the same two facts:
the board reached the run's end status, and the run attached a new or updated
task-scoped outcome artifact. Newly named producer resources count, including
`<ELEMENT>_change-request_rev<N>.md`; the exact runtime-written
`<ELEMENT>_change-request_rev<N>.patch` and `-validation.log` forms do not. A
run that attached nothing keeps its board status, records the refusal, blocks a
reviewer launch, and leaves no Change Request. A read that FAILS is reported as
a failure and never as "did not hand off".

A workspace-claiming producer that publishes nothing says why, on the run's event
log and in a board note: `handoff_unsatisfied`, with the detail — the board is
not at the end status, or no task-scoped outcome artifact was attached.

The other two reasons are recorded nowhere, on one rule: a reason that is true of
every run of its class says nothing about the run in front of you, and repeating
it on every completion buries the case the reporting exists for. An un-isolated
run skips with `no_managed_workspace`, which is true of every un-isolated run. A
reviewer-archetype run skips with `not_a_producer`, which is true of every
reviewer run — a reviewer judges a candidate rather than publishing one, so
noting it would append a line describing correct behaviour to the element's
progress on every single review cycle.

`repository_delta=empty` means the producer changed no repository file. It is an
attribute, never a state and never terminal: the Change Request is `ready` like
any other, a reviewer **is** spawned, and the injected prompt says in plain terms
that nothing changed and lists the outcome resources that were produced. Deciding
whether that was correct is a judgement about intent versus delivery, which is
what the reviewer archetype is for.

**The kind is derived from the board, never declared.** A Change Request is
`task_delta` or `story_final`, and the second is what `worktree integrate`
accepts as the Story's integration unit. Publication asks the board whether
completing this leaf would also close its Story — the same question
`worktree checkpoint` asks to REFUSE that leaf, through the same call — so the
leaf one command declines is exactly the leaf the other treats as the Story's
unit. Every indeterminate answer refuses the publication: no board access, an
unreadable board, or an element the board does not contain means the question was
never answered, and answering `task_delta` on a failed read would leave the Story
permanently unintegrable with nothing recording why.

**The base follows the kind.** A `task_delta` is measured from the Story branch
tip, where earlier leaves already checkpointed. A `story_final` is measured from
the workspace record's **current base OID**, initially the exact selected base
OID produced by upstream freshness resolution and later advanced only by a
successful final-leaf refresh or by final publication after it proves selected
upstream authority, checkpoint, managed branch, and checked-out HEAD have one
consistent provenance. Publication accepts either the unchanged checkpoint with
an uncommitted candidate in the worktree, or a branch/HEAD exactly one direct,
single-parent commit past that checkpoint when the commit tree equals the tree
independently snapshotted from the selected base and present worktree. It is
never recomputed through the local trunk ref: that ref may remain behind the
upstream that provisioning fetched, which would expand the patch to
already-merged predecessor work. Any OID/tree/provenance mismatch refuses before
validation or partial Change Request persistence. The candidate tree is a full
snapshot, so the recorded base is also the stable input to later re-parenting
when trunk moves again.

**A Story has at most one non-checkpointed producer candidate.** The worktree is
Story-scoped and the snapshotter has no durable per-task path ownership model.
Before a producer starts, spawn therefore scans the Story's Change Requests and
refuses `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` when another element owns a
`draft`, `ready`, `reviewing`, `changes_requested`, `stale`, `conflicted`, or
`accepted` revision with repository work still exposed. A real-delta
`accepted` revision blocks until `checkpointed`; a proved empty-delta accepted
revision does not block, and `checkpointed` does not block. The refusal names
the blocking task and Change Request and is
checked in two places: in the spawn command before provisioning (and again
after lease admission there), and in the runner's slot walk after lease
admission and before `slot_claimed`, which is the only path that closes the
concurrent-spawn race for queued runs. Reviewer-archetype runs bypass the
producer gate, as does the integration run bound to the element's own accepted
revision, and the same element may start rework for its own revision. A failed
Change Request store read is a refusal that keeps a queued run queued and is
never treated as no sibling.

One exception, extended by `BUG-260910-1txum4`: a `ready`, `reviewing`, or
`accepted` revision whose `repository_delta` is `empty` and whose
`changed_paths` is empty does NOT refuse a sibling. It carries no
repository work product for the new producer's writes to contaminate, its
checkpoint creates no commit, and blocking on it deadlocked any Story whose
analyst run published a zero-path candidate no reviewer was ever routed at. The
four states that expect a REPUBLISH — `draft`, `changes_requested`, `stale`,
`conflicted` — keep refusing whatever their delta is, because the next snapshot
of the shared worktree would pick up the sibling's writes. A record with no
readable `repository_delta`, or whose empty label contradicts its changed-path
list, refuses too. See
[change request lifecycle](change-request-lifecycle.md).

An accepted empty-delta leaf whose deliverable landed outside this repository
is closed explicitly with
`task-board m 'integrate_external(<ID>, evidence="<landing evidence>")'`.
The mutation admits only an element at `integrating` with the current CR at
`accepted`, `repository_delta=empty`, and zero changed paths. It records the
evidence in the immutable CR and element notes, marks the CR integrated by
evidence, moves only that leaf to `done`, and recalculates its Story aggregate.
A real delta, an unaccepted revision, or any board status other than
`integrating` refuses without writes.

**The producer runs the landing suite and records tree-bound evidence.** When
`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` is configured, publication runs it
in the Story worktree and records `validation` keyed to the published candidate
tree. The suite runs against the WORKTREE while the evidence claims a TREE, so
the candidate is recomputed afterwards and compared; a suite that moved the
worktree — a build that drops artifacts, a codegen step, a formatter — yields
**no** evidence rather than evidence keyed to a tree it never saw. A failing
suite is fail-sticky at its first non-zero command and cannot publish a `ready`
candidate: the immutable revision is immediately `changes_requested`, its
# Attack-Surface Catalog

Shared by the producer and the reviewer so the review round cannot be
narrower than the candidate. Keyed by **surface type**, not by leaf: a leaf
that spans more than one of these rows is carrying more than one attack
surface and is decomposed before spawn. The fixed attack-family list per row
is seeded from the issue #296 evidence table (revisions 1–19, finding
classes F1–F4); the reviewer does not derive the table alone and the
producer cannot negotiate a row away.

A leaf's surface table is a **subset** of these rows: the brief names the
rows the leaf touches, in highest-risk-first order, and every named row gets
exactly one result (`held`, `broken`, or `not-attacked`) under the Review
Round Contract in `.roles/reviewer/role.md`. The table is a floor, not a
ceiling — the free-hunt budget after the sweep is spent beyond it, and what
the free hunt finds becomes a new catalog row or a new family on an existing
row.

## read boundary

**Invariant:** no open or listing outside the resolved root, observed by an
audit hook.

**Attack families:**

- **symlink out** — a member path that resolves outside the source root is
  read or listed.
- **symlink cycle** — a cyclic link raises an unstructured failure instead of
  a structured refusal.
- **hard link** — a hard link inside the root names bytes outside it.
- **mount point** — a mount inside the root exposes another filesystem.
- **case variant** — a `SKILL.MD` / `.GIT` spelling resolves where the
  canonical one would not, or vice versa.
- **NFC/NFD** — canonically equivalent names resolve to different members.
- **directory swapped for a link mid-walk** — a TOCTOU swap between listing
  and opening.
- **permission error mid-walk** — an unreadable directory aborts the walk or
  leaks raw instead of refusing structurally.
- **a second walker** — two enumeration paths where the boundary check covers
  only one.

**Seeded by:** issue #296, class F1 (read boundary): rev 1 (symlinked
SKILL.md read), rev 15 (pruned subtrees still walked by the second walker),
rev 16 (cyclic symlink raises `RuntimeError`), rev 17 (`PermissionError`
leaks raw; failed `samefile` probe read as "no boundary").

## managed-output pruning

**Invariant:** physical ancestry against resolved output roots.

**Attack families:**

- **case variants** — an output root matched lexically while the filesystem
  folds case.
- **nested workspaces** — ancestry through a workspace nested inside another.
- **output root is a link** — the root itself resolves elsewhere.
- **output root inside a member** — the pruned tree overlaps an enumerated
  member.
- **unreadable root** — a root that cannot be stated or resolved is treated
  as absent instead of failing closed.

**Seeded by:** issue #296, class F2 (output boundary): rev 1 (`.GIT` case
variant on a case-insensitive filesystem), rev 2 (roots compared, not
descendants), rev 3 (nested-workspace ancestry), rev 17 (failed probe read
as "no boundary"), rev 18 (`realpath(strict=False)` swallows errors below
the wrapper), rev 19 (lexical containment bypassed by a case-variant home
spelling).

## selector paths

**Invariant:** explicit selection and wildcard discovery share one boundary
path.

**Attack families:**

- **individual selector** — one member named explicitly, bypassing the
  discovery-time check.
- **literal include** — an include list naming members outside the boundary.
- **glob** — a pattern expanding across the boundary.
- **dotfile** — dot-members included or skipped inconsistently between the
  two paths.
- **empty selector** — an empty selection matching everything or nothing.

**Seeded by:** issue #296, class F2 (output boundary): rev 4 (explicit
selection bypasses the boundary that only wildcard discovery checked).

## text grammar

**Invariant:** accept only the named subset; accepted means values equal the
reference.

**Attack families:**

- **BOM and encodings** — a byte-order mark or a non-UTF-8 encoding accepted
  or misread.
- **line endings** — CR, lone CR, or mixed endings changing parse outcome.
- **control characters** — raw U+0000–U+001F / U+007F accepted in values.
- **Unicode whitespace** — non-ASCII space treated as blank, or rewritten.
- **comments** — trailing or full-line comments kept as values, or valid
  values refused as unterminated.
- **quotes and escapes** — the escape alphabet over- or under-accepts.
- **block scalars** — chomping, indentation indicators, or header comments
  mishandled.
- **numeric-looking scalars** — `null`, `{}`, `0x2A`, or huge ints coerced to
  the wrong type.
- **duplicate keys** — a repeated key silently wins, loses, or hides behind
  framing.
- **size limits** — an oversized document accepted without bound.

**Seeded by:** issue #296, class F3 (parser): rev 1 (`null`/`{}`/`true`/`42`
as strings), rev 2 (`0x2A`/`0o52` as strings), rev 3 (5000-digit int;
TAB before `#`), rev 4 (`description: # comment`; `"valid" # comment`),
rev 5 (whitespace-only line chomped), rev 6 (indented `---` fence),
rev 7 (indent indicator; header comment), rev 8 (chomping before baseline
removal), rev 9 (raw control characters), rev 10 (Unicode whitespace;
U+2028/U+2029 rewritten), rev 11 (trailing NEL/LS/PS), rev 12
(`"x"#comment`; body lone CR), rev 14 (`\/` and literal TAB escapes).

## identity

**Invariant:** identity is a function of raw bytes.

**Attack families:**

- **any normalisation** — Unicode or whitespace normalisation applied before
  hashing.
- **any line-ending rewrite** — CR, NEL, LS, or PS rewritten before hashing.
- **trailing bytes** — bytes after the closing fence included or dropped
  inconsistently.

**Seeded by:** issue #296, class F3 rev 11 (trailing NEL/LS/PS still
normalised, changing package identity) and class F4 (framing): rev 12 (body
lone CR), rev 13 (CR at EOF after the closing fence normalised away).

## error surface

**Invariant:** every failure through a public entry point is a structured
refusal.

**Attack families:**

- **PermissionError** — unreadable member or directory leaks raw.
- **OSError** — I/O failures surface unstructured.
- **RecursionError** — deep or cyclic trees escape as interpreter errors.
- **RuntimeError** — internal failures (for example cycle detection) escape
  unshaped.
- **UnicodeDecodeError** — undecodable bytes crash instead of refusing.

**Seeded by:** issue #296: F1 rev 16 (cyclic symlink raises `RuntimeError`),
F1/F2 rev 17 (`PermissionError` leaks raw), F2 rev 18 (errors swallowed
below the wrapper, so the fail-closed wrapper never fires).

## ordering

**Invariant:** byte order independent of locale and filesystem.

**Attack families:**

- **non-ASCII names** — ordering shifts with locale collation.
- **NFC/NFD pairs** — canonically equivalent names order inconsistently.
- **case pairs** — case variants order inconsistently across filesystems.

**Seeded by:** preventive row — no reproduced defect in revs 1–19. The
families guard the byte-order invariant the acceptance criteria state, so a
first ordering defect arrives as a `broken` row rather than a new class.

## destination conflicts

**Invariant:** the conflict check uses the same identity as installation.

**Attack families:**

- **case-insensitive collisions** — two names differing only in case install
  over each other.
- **NFC/NFD collisions** — canonically equivalent names install over each
  other.
- **existing link at destination** — a link already at the destination
  redirects the install.

**Seeded by:** preventive row — no reproduced defect in revs 1–19. The
families pin the check to installation identity, so a first collision
defect arrives as a `broken` row rather than a new class.

## Coverage map

The producer-side coverage map for a leaf's surface table follows the
template in [coverage-map-template.md](coverage-map-template.md): one row
per catalog surface with the attacking tests, the killed narrowing
mutants, and the out-of-contract inputs with their AC clause.

