### Review verdict findings

A review verdict resource (`<ELEMENT-ID>_review-verdict-rev<N>.md`) is prose
first: the reviewer writes the human-readable verdict as markdown. The
machine-readable finding set lives in exactly one fenced block with the info
string `verdict-findings`, holding one JSON object with the REQUIRED
`findings` array plus the separate `notes` list:

````markdown
```verdict-findings
{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "row": "managed-output pruning",
      "invariant": "physical ancestry against resolved output roots",
      "mechanism": "prune.go:44 compares output roots, not descendants",
      "reproductions": [
        {
          "test_file": "prune_test.go",
          "command": "go test ./prune/ -run TestNestedWorkspace",
          "expected_failure": "nested workspace member is listed",
          "pinned_blobs": ["sha256:abc..."]
        }
      ],
      "severity": "bypass",
      "repeat-of": "none"
    }
  ],
  "notes": ["suspicion: tab handling unreviewed"],
  "surface_results": [
    {"row": "managed-output pruning", "result": "broken"},
    {"row": "text grammar", "result": "broken"},
    {"row": "read boundary", "result": "held"}
  ],
  "free_hunt": []
}
```
````

One finding is one mechanism. Field contract:

- `id` — class slug, stable across revisions, reused when the mechanism
  repeats. Two findings of one class in one verdict legitimately share it;
  findings deduplicate by mechanism, not by id. A slug carries no whitespace
  or control characters — the verdict is refused otherwise — because the id
  interpolates into the loop detector's line-oriented note.
- `row` — the surface-table row the attack broke.
- `invariant` — the row invariant that did not hold.
- `mechanism` — the file and line, or a one-sentence design statement.
- `reproductions` — one or more executable reproductions against the exact
  candidate blobs. Each names `test_file`, `command`, `expected_failure`,
  and the non-empty `pinned_blobs` digest list. A suspicion with no failing
  reproduction is a note, never a finding.
- `severity` — exactly one of `bypass`, `regression`, `robustness`, `note`
  (lowercase). Only the first three block.
- `repeat-of` — the prior revision and finding id this mechanism repeats,
  spelled `rev<N>/<id>` (for example `rev2/F1-framing`), or `none` when the
  mechanism is new. The key keeps its literal hyphenated spelling. The
  loop-detector S2 continues the named id's class when that id is present in
  the immediately previous verdict; any other value — free text, a
  misspelling, an id from a dormant revision — continues nothing and the
  class is the finding's own id alone.
- `ac_unclassifiable` — OPTIONAL boolean, default `false`. `true` when the
  reviewer judges the acceptance criteria cannot classify this finding's
  input as in or out of scope. The finding is still recorded in-spec — a
  blocking severity still blocks — and loop-detector signal S5 fires on it
  (see [change-request lifecycle](change-request-lifecycle.md#loop-detector-s1-s2-s3-s4-s5)).
  Verdicts written before this key existed decode `false`.
- `surface_results` — OPTIONAL, one result per surface-table row walked:
  `{"row": "<table row>", "result": "held|broken|not-attacked"}`. An
  optional `detail` states why a row is `not-attacked` (budget, a broken
  row it depends on, or a missing fixture) and carries no gate meaning.
- `free_hunt` — OPTIONAL, the free-hunt findings beyond the table as a
  string list. Acceptance requires it empty.

`notes` is a list of suspicion strings. Notes never block and are not
counted. An empty `findings` array is valid shape — an accept verdict
carries no blocking findings — but the `changes_requested` route requires at
least one finding, because a rejection with no findings rejects nothing.

Shape rules, enforced by both verdict routes (`accept_cr` and `reject_cr`)
with the typed `verdict_findings_invalid` refusal:

- zero `verdict-findings` blocks is a LEGACY prose verdict, not an empty or
  malformed one: it loads and routes as before and records no findings;
- more than one block, an unterminated block, or a block that is not exactly
  one JSON object is malformed;
- a fenced block whose info string names a verdict but is not exactly
  `verdict-findings` is malformed rather than absent — a near-miss fence is
  evidence the reviewer meant to carry findings, and reading evident intent
  as absence would silently drop the set;
- every finding and every reproduction is validated, not just the first;
- a present `surface_results` list is exact: every entry names a row and one
  result word (`held`, `broken`, `not-attacked`, lowercase), and every row
  appears at most once. Absence is valid shape — pre-sweep verdicts carry
  no list — but absence beside a surface table does not sweep it;
- every `free_hunt` entry is a non-empty string;
- unknown keys are ignored, so a newer writer does not refuse an older
  reader — but a misspelled required key (for example `repeat_of`) fails the
  presence check for the canonical spelling.

The Change Request store records the parsed verdict beside the revision as
`verdict_findings`, and a `changes_requested` verdict with N findings
produces exactly one `to-dev` transition for the whole set. What a finding
MEANS — the round contract, severity criteria, the out-of-contract clause,
and the repeat rule — lives in the reviewer role
(`.roles/reviewer/role.md`, section `Review Round Contract`) and in
[change-request lifecycle](change-request-lifecycle.md#two-same-class-findings-mean-a-missing-regression-in-the-owning-leaf),
drops what does not reproduce with a note, merges by mechanism, ranks by
severity, spends the remaining budget on a free hunt, and records the
single verdict; its prompt carries the merged analyst draft in a
`Verdict Run Brief`. Only the verdict run handed the revision may
`accept_cr` or `reject_cr`. The analyst and verdict-run brief templates are in
[sub-agent templates](sub-agent-templates.md#review-analyst-fan-out-one-run-per-surface-row)
and
[sub-agent templates](sub-agent-templates.md#review-verdict-run-merge-re-run-one-verdict).

## Loop detector: S1, S2, S3, S4, S5

Every `changes_requested` verdict recording evaluates five loop-detector
signals over the leaf's revision history — the CR revision records plus the
verdict findings arrays stamped beside them, the stored per-revision
candidate patches, and the acceptance snapshots recorded at publication —
and records the outcome on the element as a finding-class note. There are
two recording routes and both evaluate: `reject_cr`, the reviewer-called
mutation, which stamps the verdict findings; and the spawn runtime's
`recordChangeRequestVerdict`, which moves the revision when a reviewer run
completes at `to-dev` without calling `reject_cr` and stamps nothing — the
runtime observes only the verdict branch, so S1 decides on the revision
count and S4 on the stored patches and snapshots there, while S2, S3 and S5
state their bound. What the orchestrator DOES with a fired signal — the
response routing and the spawn gate — is the loop response below. This
section names all five signals, their exact thresholds, the note format,
and the response each recording routes to.

- **S1, revision overrun.** Fires when the leaf's revision count strictly
  exceeds `max(3, 2 x median revisions of the accepted leaves in the same
  Story)`. The median runs over Task/Bug siblings under the same parent whose
  Change Request is accepted or beyond, and doubles exactly: twice the middle

